--TEST--
`[log] format = "text"` selects `rule:errors/renderings`'s second rendering for the target, and the producer that built the record still names no rendering at all — `rule:errors/renderings` and `rule:errors/record-producers`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// `rule:errors/renderings`'s table gives a log target two of the three renderings, and § 3
// says outright that no producer takes a format argument. So this program is
// the JSON Lines cases beside it, character for character in its source: what
// differs is one line of `nvs.toml`, which is the whole claim.
Core\Script::onExit(fn (): void => {
    echo Core\IO::readText("app.log");
});

// The envelope's header line, then the bag as one indented, typed line per
// field — `nvs_render::plain`'s layout, which is `var_dump`'s in shape.
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Error, "the application said so", ["user" => 7]);

// A second record, so the rendering is asked of the block *boundary* as well:
// a plaintext record is a run of lines with no blank one between records, which
// is why the JSON Lines default is what an unconfigured target still emits.
Core\Log::write(Core\Log\Level::Warn, "and again");
--FILE nvs.toml--
[log]
target = "file:app.log"
format = "text"

[capabilities.fs]
read = ["."]
--EXPECT--
[error] the application said so
  user => int(7)
[warn] and again
